[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: An empty room with light wooden floors and white walls.
AUDIO: 'So, there are a lot of ways you can use AI [music] in real estate videos right now, good or bad, but I don\'t think they quite all have the same value. Some of them are almost completely invisible. They\'re more just practical fixes to make your footage look a little bit better. And then on the other end, you'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can use AI to create some shots that would literally be impossible to film. So, today I\'m going through seven levels of AI for real estate videos, starting with probably the most simple and most practical use case, and working our'

[00:24] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: A small bathroom with a sink, toilet, and a mirror reflecting the videographer.
AUDIO: '[music] way up to some pretty crazy stuff. And level one is something that has annoyed me pretty much every time when I [music] filmed a bathroom. Whenever you\'re filming a bathroom, you\'re usually fighting the mirror. Usually, you can find a really nice'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'composition, you start moving through the shot, and then you realize you\'re standing right in the middle of the reflection. Normally, that means changing the angle and sacrificing the composition [music] entirely, but with AI, you really don\'t need to worry about'

[00:49] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A close-up of a dark-themed UI, likely Nano Banana Pro, with text "remove the person from the photo" and a "Generate" button.
AUDIO: 'that anymore. You can just remove yourself afterwards. [music] And this is probably the least flashy thing I\'m going to show you in this entire video, but it\'s also one of my favorites because it\'s so practical. The workflow is really simple. I\'ll export a'

[01:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'frame from the shot and bring that to Nano Banana Pro. [music] Then I\'ll have it remove me from the mirror while retaining the rest of the room as closely as possible. Once I have a clean reference image [music] that I like, I\'ll bring the original video and that'

[01:14] [scene:talking head]
VISUAL: The creator, Joel Van Beek, speaking to the camera.
AUDIO: 'image into something like C Dance 2.5 edit or Cling 01 edit, and then I\'ll prompt it to retain the exact original camera movement and room details, but use the reference [music] image to remove the person from the reflection.'

[01:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And now I basically got the exact shot that I filmed, just without my beautiful self standing in the way of it. I love this type of AI because ideally nobody watching the final video even knows it was used. It just means I can use the'

[01:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'best looking angle instead of an angle that just so happens to hide me. But that\'s still [music] AI being used to fix footage. Level two is where we actually start adding something [music] to the shot. Level two is adding character to your footage. And when I'

[01:52] [scene:talking head]
VISUAL: Joel Van Beek speaking to the camera.
AUDIO: 'say adding character, I don\'t necessarily meaning literally adding a character, although sometimes I do. What I really mean is adding a little bit of life and movement to the footage that otherwise feels too clean or'

[02:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'static. [music] For example, I might add someone walking through the foreground or something as simple as turning on the tap that wasn\'t running when I originally filmed it. And if I want somebody in the shot, I can take a clean frame from the [music]'

[02:18] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A dark UI, likely DaVinci Resolve, showing "MotionBlur" controls with options "Faster" and "Better".
AUDIO: 'video along with a reference photo of the realtor or agent, or in this case myself, and then inside of C Dance 2.5 edit, I can have that person walk across the frame parallel to [music] the camera. And straight out of AI, it might look a little bit too obvious, so back'

[02:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in Resolve, I\'ll usually stylize it [music] with some heavy motion blur or frame blending. That way the person feels like a natural foreground element instead of something you\'re supposed to stare at and inspect. [music] And for something simpler, I can upload a shot'

[02:44] [scene:talking head]
VISUAL: Joel Van Beek speaking to the camera.
AUDIO: 'of a faucet and have C Dance retain the original camera movement while adding running water. I really like this because it adds some life to the video without completely changing the property. [music] And it can also save you having to stage every little detail'

[02:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'while you\'re filming, but one other thing that\'s considerably harder to fake practically is shooting the exact same property twice &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; at completely different times of the day. So, level three is day-to-night. I love day-to-night transitions in real'

[03:10] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DaVinci Resolve interface showing a file menu with "Quick Export" highlighted and video thumbnails.
AUDIO: 'estate videos because they show off the entire exterior in a completely different setting and make the video a whole lot more cinematic. But the annoying part is actually filming them. You usually need to film the entire'

[03:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'property during the day, and then you need to come back [music] and do the whole process again. But AI makes this way easier. Usually, I\'ll take a frame from the daytime shot and bring that into [music] Nano Banana Pro. Then, I\'ll generate the same property at night,'

[03:35] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A large house with a green lawn and mountains in the background, transitioning from day to night.
AUDIO: 'keeping the architecture and landscaping consistent while turning on the interior and exterior lights. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; From there, I can take the original video and that nighttime reference into C Dance 2.5 edit or Clingo 1 edit and generate a nighttime version while'

[03:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'retaining the same [music] camera movement. And one important thing here is that I actually don\'t like asking AI to create the transition from day to night. I\'d rather have my clean daytime clip and then generate one clean nighttime clip and control the actual'

[04:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'transition myself. Back inside of DaVinci Resolve, one of my favorite ways to blend these together is with my Luma Fade transition. And sometimes I\'ll add a little camera shake on top of that, and now you\'ve got a really nice looking transition from day to night. This way I'

[04:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have way more control over the timing and the style of the edit instead of relying on AI to do everything for me. But so far we\'ve still started with footage that I actually remember to capture, and level four solves one of my most common problems when I get back'

[04:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from a shoot. [music] And this is generating detail shots that you\'ve missed. I\'ve definitely finished filming a house, started editing, and realized that I could probably use one or two more close-up shots of a kitchen or a bathroom or a bedroom, [music]'

[04:42] [scene:talking head]
VISUAL: Joel Van Beek speaking to the camera.
AUDIO: 'but AI is getting surprisingly good at filling those little gaps. Let\'s say I have a wider shot of the faucet, but I never [music] filmed a proper close-up. I\'ll grab the best frame that I have showing the faucet, and ideally another'

[04:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'angle or two if I happen to have it. Then inside of Cinema Studio 4.0, I can use Nano Banana Pro to generate a new close-up while still referencing [music] the actual object in the house. This is where I define things like the camera'

[05:06] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A UI, likely Cinema Studio 4.0, displaying various camera movement options like "Whip pan", "Slow zoom in", and "Arc left".
AUDIO: 'angle and also how shallow of a depth of field that I want. Once I like the image, I can switch over to the video mode and use that generated image as a reference. [music] For this type of shot, I usually like to stick with the modern camera with a clean sharp lens,'

[05:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and I\'ll open the aperture up all the way to F 1.4. Then I can choose the exact camera movement I want, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; and now I have that missing detail shot without having to go back to the property. And obviously, I\'m not using'

[05:31] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A spacious living room with large windows and modern furniture.
AUDIO: 'this to invent new features that aren\'t actually there or change the features that are. I\'m just using this to generate another angle of something that\'s really in the house, and it\'s incredibly useful. But up until now, we\'ve mostly [music] been focusing on'

[05:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just showing the property itself and level five starts selling a little bit more of the lifestyle around it. An empty room can look beautiful, but sometimes it still feels a little lifeless. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Adding some subtle lifestyle imagery can help sell what it actually feels like to'

[05:58] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A UI, likely C Dance, showing video generation settings with a prompt "Camera should slowly be pushing in with smooth motion. A family is playing a board game."
AUDIO: 'live in the space instead of only showing the architecture. [music] I\'ll start with a real frame from the property and use C Dream 5.0 to generate a believable lifestyle image. I\'ll describe [music] who and what should be in the scene, what they\'re doing, and'

[06:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'make sure they fit naturally into the lighting and perspective of the room. Once I have an image that I like, I can take that into something like Kling or C Dance 2.0 and use that still as a starting frame. Then, I\'ll keep the animation pretty restrained. You usually'

[06:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'don\'t need something dramatically moving around the house. Usually, a small amount of natural motion is completely enough to sell the feeling of the shot. But, we\'re still working with a room that\'s already finished. Level six is'

[06:36] [scene:talking head]
VISUAL: Joel Van Beek speaking to the camera.
AUDIO: 'what I use when the room is completely [music] empty. I\'ve shown AI furniture staging quite a few times on my channel before, but I think it still deserves a spot pretty high on this list, especially if you\'re filming a vacant'

[06:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'property. My [music] first approach is to grab a frame from the beginning of the clip and one at the very end of the clip. Then, I\'ll take that end frame into Nano Banana Pro and generate the fully furnished version of that room. From there, I can put the original frame'

[07:02] [scene:talking head]
VISUAL: Joel Van Beek speaking to the camera.
AUDIO: 'and the furnished [music] frame into Kling 3.0 and animate between them. Or, if I want a little bit more control over how the furniture actually [music] appears, I can use C Dance 2.5 edit with the furnished image as a visual'

[07:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reference and [music] prompt the individual pieces into the room. And this is where I think AI staging gets much more interesting than traditional virtual staging. Instead [music] of showing somebody a still image of what the room could look like furnished, the transformation can actually become a'

[07:27] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A modern bathroom with a freestanding tub and large windows.
AUDIO: 'huge part of the video. But, level seven takes that same basic concept, but uses it to visualize something that you usually can\'t see at all. And now, we\'re going to focus on what\'s actually hidden. [music] This bathroom has heated'

[07:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'floors, which is a great feature, but it\'s pretty difficult to demonstrate that through a video because obviously you can\'t see the heat underneath [music] the tile. So, instead we can use AI to reveal what\'s actually hidden'

[07:51] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: A UI, likely Higgsfield, showing options to add elements or references, and a prompt for revealing heated floor elements.
AUDIO: 'underneath the floor. This workflow [music] is very similar to the furniture staging we just did, so there\'s no reason to go through every single step again. I\'ll take the frames from the shot, use Nano Banana Pro to generate the x-ray style version that reveals the'

[08:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'heating system underneath, then I can use Kling 3.0 to animate between the real floor and the x-ray view. Or I can do the exact same thing with C Dance 2.5 edit and just use the reference image instead. And this is where AI starts'

[08:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'doing something that normal cameras simply can\'t. Instead of just making the video more visually interesting, we\'re actually visualizing a feature that the home has that otherwise would completely be hidden unless you were actually in the room. And I think that\'s actually'

[08:29] [scene:talking head]
VISUAL: Joel Van Beek speaking to the camera.
AUDIO: 'one of the most interesting directions that this technology can go. So, those are my seven levels of using AI in real estate videos. And what I like about this whole progression is that AI doesn\'t have to mean completely'

[08:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'generating fake real estate videos. Some of my favorite use cases are really practical, removing myself from a mirror, fixing a shot that feels lifeless, and adding a little bit of extra detail when I forgot to film. And then if the project calls for it, you'

[08:54] [scene:talking head]
VISUAL: Joel Van Beek speaking to the camera.
AUDIO: 'can push things much further with AI transformations and visualizing features that can\'t actually be captured on camera. [music] The way I look at it is the further that you go with AI, the more intentional you actually need to be about using it. If it helps communicate'

[09:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the property better or lets you create something that genuinely couldn\'t be captured with a camera, that\'s where I think it becomes really powerful. Thank you guys so much for watching and if you have any questions, make sure that you drop them in the comment section down'

[09:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'below. And as always, Jesus loves you. Peace.'
